Builder Design Pattern
======================
The Builder Design Pattern is a creational pattern used to construct complex objects step-by-step. 
It provides better control over the construction process and is especially useful when an object 
requires many optional fields or configurations.

✅ Why use Builder Pattern?
To create immutable objects with many optional parameters.
Avoid telescoping constructors (too many constructors with different combinations of parameters).
Improves code readability and maintainability.

💡 Real-world Analogy
Think of building a burger: you can choose the bun, patty, toppings, sauces, etc. A builder helps you 
configure and build the burger exactly the way you want.

Singleton Design Pattern in Java
====================================
The Singleton Pattern is a creational design pattern that ensures a class has only one instance 
throughout the application and provides a global point of access to it.
🔧 Use Cases
Logger
Configuration manager
Database connection pool
Cache
🧠 Key Concepts
Private constructor – Prevents other classes from instantiating it.
Static instance – Holds the only instance.
Public static method – Returns the instance.

Factory Design Pattern
=======================
The Factory Design Pattern is a creational design pattern used in Java to create objects without 
exposing the creation logic to the client. Instead of calling a constructor directly, you use a factory 
method that returns an instance of a class.

✅ Why use Factory Pattern?
To handle object creation logic centrally.
To return different subclasses based on input.
Promotes loose coupling and code flexibility.

🔄 Iterator Design Pattern in Java
==================================
The Iterator Design Pattern is a behavioral design pattern that provides a way to access elements of
a collection sequentially without exposing its internal structure.
✅ Why use Iterator Pattern?
To traverse a collection without exposing how it's implemented.
To provide a uniform interface for different types of collections.
To support multiple iterations over a collection.
🧠 Key Concepts:
Iterator: Interface with methods like hasNext() and next().
Concrete Iterator: Implements traversal logic.
Aggregate (Collection): The data structure being traversed.


